  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through Friday) \nIssued at 850 PM CDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nThe long term period will be largely dictated by the placement and\ndepth of the upper level trough shifting eastward into the\nAppalachian region early next week. Models continue to have\ndisagreements, however, the general consensus so far is that\nlingering low pressure over the southeastern U.S. will create daily\nlow chances (20% or less) of showers/thunderstorms with the \nassistance of diurnal heating through mid next week. If any\nthunderstorms are realized, the only day that could support a low\nchance of stronger storms at this time would be Wednesday with the \nLREF showing a decent amount of instability (Sfc CAPE > 1000 J/kg) \nalong with generally unimpressive shear. Otherwise, a gradual warming\npattern will be observed through next week with highs reaching the\nmid 90s by Thursday and Friday along with overnight lows in the\n60s/70s.",
